      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:australian-capital-territory-supreme-court-act-1933-sec-26",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "26 Mandamus, injunctions and receivers",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-capital-territory-supreme-court-act-1933#sec-26"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "australian-capital-territory-supreme-court-act-1933.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "australian-capital-territory-supreme-court-act-1933.pdf, p.22",
      "values:originalText": "(1) The Supreme Court may grant a mandamus or an injunction or\nappoint a receiver by any interlocutory order in all cases in which it\nappears to the Court to be just or convenient so to do.\n\n(2) Any such order may be made either unconditionally or on such\nterms and conditions as the Court thinks just.\n\n(3) If, whether before, or at, or after the hearing of any cause or matter,\nan application is made for an injunction to prevent any threatened\nor apprehended waste or trespass, the injunction may be granted, if\nthe Court thinks fit, whether the person against whom the\ninjunction is sought is or is not in possession under any claim of\ntitle or otherwise, or (if out of possession) does or does not claim a\nright to do the act sought to be restrained under any colour of title,\nand whether the estates claimed by both or by either of the parties\nare legal or equitable.\n\n\nConstitution and Jurisdiction of the Supreme Court  Part II\n\nSection 27\n\nAustralian Capital Territory Supreme Court Act 1933",
      "skos:definition": "(1) The Supreme Court may grant a mandamus or an injunction or\nappoint a receiver by any interlocutory order in all cases in which it\nappears to the Court to be just or convenient so to do.\n\n(2) Any such order may be made either unconditionally or on such\nterms and conditions as the Court thinks just.\n\n(3) If, whether before, or at, or after the hearing of any cause or matter,\nan application is made for an injunction to prevent any threatened\nor apprehended waste or trespass, the injunction may be granted, if\nthe Court thinks fit, whether the person against whom the\ninjunction is sought is or is not in possession under any claim of\ntitle or otherwise, or (if out of possession) does or does not claim a\nright to do the act sought to be restrained under any colour of title,\nand whether the estates claimed by both or by either of the parties\nare legal or equitable.\n\n\nConstitution and Jurisdiction of the Supreme Court  Part II\n\nSection 27\n\nAustralian Capital Territory Supreme Court Act 1933",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-capital-territory-supreme-court-act-1933-sec-26-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-capital-territory-supreme-court-act-1933-sec-26-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-capital-territory-supreme-court-act-1933-sec-26-ss-3"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "807b56eacb0ba48f20f70099f1855e6bc1e521b63a31743c503bf76ee1a94ce1"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-capital-territory-supreme-court-act-1933#sec-26",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "26 Mandamus, injunctions and receivers",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 22,
      "values:originalText": "(1) The Supreme Court may grant a mandamus or an injunction or\nappoint a receiver by any interlocutory order in all cases in which it\nappears to the Court to be just or convenient so to do.\n\n(2) Any such order may be made either unconditionally or on such\nterms and conditions as the Court thinks just.\n\n(3) If, whether before, or at, or after the hearing of any cause or matter,\nan application is made for an injunction to prevent any threatened\nor apprehended waste or trespass, the injunction may be granted, if\nthe Court thinks fit, whether the person against whom the\ninjunction is sought is or is not in possession under any claim of\ntitle or otherwise, or (if out of possession) does or does not claim a\nright to do the act sought to be restrained under any colour of title,\nand whether the estates claimed by both or by either of the parties\nare legal or equitable.\n\n\nConstitution and Jurisdiction of the Supreme Court  Part II\n\nSection 27\n\nAustralian Capital Territory Supreme Court Act 1933"
    },
    {
