    {
      "@id": "concept:australian-capital-territory-supreme-court-act-1933-sec-42",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "42 Non-appearance or absence of some defendants",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-capital-territory-supreme-court-act-1933#sec-42"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "australian-capital-territory-supreme-court-act-1933.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "australian-capital-territory-supreme-court-act-1933.pdf, p.34",
      "values:originalText": "(1) When there are several defendants in any cause pending in the\nSupreme Court, if any defendant is not served with process and\ndoes not voluntarily appear, the Court may nevertheless entertain\nthe cause and proceed to hear and determine it between the parties\nwho are properly before the Court; but the judgment given in the\ncause shall not conclude or prejudice other parties who are not\nregularly served with process and do not voluntarily submit to the\njurisdiction of the Court.\n\n(2) When, in any suit of which the Supreme Court has jurisdiction, any\ndefendant is not a resident of, or found within, the Commonwealth,\nand does not voluntarily appear in the suit, the Court may\nnevertheless proceed to exercise its jurisdiction after such notice to\nthe defendant and upon such terms as are prescribed by Rules of\nCourt.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) When there are several defendants in any cause pending in the\nSupreme Court, if any defendant is not served with process and\ndoes not voluntarily appear, the Court may nevertheless entertain\nthe cause and proceed to hear and determine it between the parties\nwho are properly before the Court; but the judgment given in the\ncause shall not conclude or prejudice other parties who are not\nregularly served with process and do not voluntarily submit to the\njurisdiction of the Court.\n\n(2) When, in any suit of which the Supreme Court has jurisdiction, any\ndefendant is not a resident of, or found within, the Commonwealth,\nand does not voluntarily appear in the suit, the Court may\nnevertheless proceed to exercise its jurisdiction after such notice to\nthe defendant and upon such terms as are prescribed by Rules of\nCourt.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-capital-territory-supreme-court-act-1933-sec-42-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-capital-territory-supreme-court-act-1933-sec-42-ss-2"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "dbb0134a9671064940038fb5825571f4c099dc331147f975a7a7a9f09d71a2fa"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-capital-territory-supreme-court-act-1933#sec-42",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "42 Non-appearance or absence of some defendants",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 34,
      "values:originalText": "(1) When there are several defendants in any cause pending in the\nSupreme Court, if any defendant is not served with process and\ndoes not voluntarily appear, the Court may nevertheless entertain\nthe cause and proceed to hear and determine it between the parties\nwho are properly before the Court; but the judgment given in the\ncause shall not conclude or prejudice other parties who are not\nregularly served with process and do not voluntarily submit to the\njurisdiction of the Court.\n\n(2) When, in any suit of which the Supreme Court has jurisdiction, any\ndefendant is not a resident of, or found within, the Commonwealth,\nand does not voluntarily appear in the suit, the Court may\nnevertheless proceed to exercise its jurisdiction after such notice to\nthe defendant and upon such terms as are prescribed by Rules of\nCourt."
    },
