    {
      "@id": "concept:australian-capital-territory-supreme-court-act-1933-sec-44a",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "44A Reserved judgments",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-capital-territory-supreme-court-act-1933#sec-44a"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "australian-capital-territory-supreme-court-act-1933.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "australian-capital-territory-supreme-court-act-1933.pdf, p.35",
      "values:originalText": "(1) Where any proceeding, after being fully heard before a Court\nconstituted by not less than 3 Judges sitting together, is ordered to\nstand for judgment, it is not necessary that all the Judges before\nwhom it was heard be present together in Court to declare their\nopinions on the matter to which the proceedings relate, but the\nopinion of any one of them may be reduced to writing and may be\nmade public by any other of them at any subsequent sitting of the\nCourt at which judgment in the proceeding is delivered.\n\n(2) In any such case the question shall be decided in the same manner,\nand the judgment of the Court has the same force and effect, as if\nthe Judge whose opinion is so made public had been present in\ncourt and had declared his opinion in person.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) Where any proceeding, after being fully heard before a Court\nconstituted by not less than 3 Judges sitting together, is ordered to\nstand for judgment, it is not necessary that all the Judges before\nwhom it was heard be present together in Court to declare their\nopinions on the matter to which the proceedings relate, but the\nopinion of any one of them may be reduced to writing and may be\nmade public by any other of them at any subsequent sitting of the\nCourt at which judgment in the proceeding is delivered.\n\n(2) In any such case the question shall be decided in the same manner,\nand the judgment of the Court has the same force and effect, as if\nthe Judge whose opinion is so made public had been present in\ncourt and had declared his opinion in person.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-capital-territory-supreme-court-act-1933-sec-44a-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:australian-capital-territory-supreme-court-act-1933-sec-44a-ss-2"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "994ef77ad8b290b218abed9a6b5c3dccc3e6c6bf1d49c06c01e425baa98c3b37"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/australian-capital-territory-supreme-court-act-1933#sec-44a",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "44A Reserved judgments",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 35,
      "values:originalText": "(1) Where any proceeding, after being fully heard before a Court\nconstituted by not less than 3 Judges sitting together, is ordered to\nstand for judgment, it is not necessary that all the Judges before\nwhom it was heard be present together in Court to declare their\nopinions on the matter to which the proceedings relate, but the\nopinion of any one of them may be reduced to writing and may be\nmade public by any other of them at any subsequent sitting of the\nCourt at which judgment in the proceeding is delivered.\n\n(2) In any such case the question shall be decided in the same manner,\nand the judgment of the Court has the same force and effect, as if\nthe Judge whose opinion is so made public had been present in\ncourt and had declared his opinion in person."
    },
